From c839fb78c66f5afa2dd5a44015222db53844a95f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt Hargett <plaztiksyke@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 15 Jun 2026 14:28:28 +0200
Subject: fast-interp legacy-EH: fix four loader/runtime correctness bugs

Four issues in the fast-interp legacy exception-handling path, all
surfaced by the integration review of this branch.

1. SIMD-off build break (wasm_loader.c, CATCH and CATCH_ALL result
   copy emit). The `cell == 4` branch emitted
   EXT_OP_COPY_STACK_TOP_V128, but that opcode is only declared under
   `#if WASM_ENABLE_SIMDE != 0` in wasm_opcode.h, so
   `-DWAMR_BUILD_SIMD=0` failed to compile. Wrap the V128 branch in
   `#if WASM_ENABLE_SIMDE != 0 ... #endif` at both sites, mirroring the
   reserve_block_ret and local-preserve paths. `cell == 4` cannot occur
   with SIMD off, so there is no behavior change.

2. eh-stack / GC frame_ref aliasing (wasm_interp_fast.c, WASM_OP_TRY
   and RETHROW). In GC builds the runtime eh-stack pointer was computed
   as `frame_lp + param + local + max_stack`, which is exactly where
   `frame->frame_ref` (the GC root bitmap) starts, so pushing a TRY
   entry corrupted GC roots. Frame setup reserves the eh-stack region
   *after* the frame_ref bitmap, so the pointer must skip the bitmap's
   `(cell_num_of_local_stack + 3) / 4` cells in GC builds. Centralize
   the offset in an EH_STACK_BASE macro that adds the bitmap term only
   under WASM_ENABLE_GC; non-GC builds are byte-for-byte unchanged.

3. Offset-stack underflow on polymorphic THROW (wasm_loader.c,
   WASM_OP_THROW operand emit). The source offsets were read from
   `frame_offset - param_cell_num` before the stack-depth validation,
   so stack-polymorphic code such as `unreachable; throw $tag_with_i32`
   underflowed the offset stack at load time. Gate the read on the same
   polymorphic short-circuit the block-param path uses and emit zeroed
   placeholder source offsets when the params are not really on the
   stack, keeping pass-1 / pass-2 size accounting balanced (the THROW
   is unreachable at runtime).

4. Per-iteration eh-stack leak via enclosing loop (wasm_loader.c, BR /
   BR_IF / BR_TABLE). The guard only rejected a try-crossing br whose
   immediate target was a loop entry, but the same per-iteration leak
   occurs when the br (or its target) sits inside an enclosing loop,
   e.g. `loop { block { try { br $block } catch_all end } br $loop }`.
   Each iteration re-pushes a TRY entry and eventually overruns the
   static `exception_handler_count * EH_ENTRY_CELLS` reservation.
   Replace the immediate-target check with a walk of the control frames
   the br does not exit (target frame down to the function frame) for an
   enclosing LOOP; a br that exits the loop entirely lands above the
   loop frame and is still accepted.

Verified by building product-mini/platforms/darwin twice with
fast-interp + exception-handling: once with SIMD off (proves #1) and
once with GC + ref-types + SIMD off (proves #2 compiles).

(cherry picked from commit 600c6e878925bcfceb73270ea33faa40aad8c1cb)
